Q: How to change HwndSource window size C# A: <p>You are adding <code>WS_CHILD</code> to the style of the window you are creating, so you intend it to be a child window of an existing WPF window.</p>
<p>In this case, the question of how to change the size of the <code>HwndSource</code> is a non-question: you <em><strong>do not</strong></em> want to change its size. WPF will do its normal layout processing, and it will decide the size that it should have.</p>
<p>If you want to have control over the layout, you have to make proper use of <code>StackPanel</code>, <code>DockPanel</code>, <code>Grid</code>, etc.  The fact that it is a <code>HwndSource</code> is irrelevant: you can replace it with a <code>Border</code>, experiment with the layout, and once you have that part right, you can replace the <code>Border</code> with the <code>HwndSource</code> and it should be positioned and sized exactly the same way.</p>
<h2>Unless you want your app to also work correctly on any DPI.</h2>
<p>For some unfathomable reason, <code>HwndSource</code> does not seem to be correctly handling the sizing of the native window with respect to DPI. To overcome this problem, add the following method to your class:</p>
<pre><code>using Wpf = System.Windows;
using WpfMedia = System.Windows.Media;

protected override void OnWindowPositionChanged( Wpf.Rect rect )
{
    Wpf.PresentationSource source = Wpf.PresentationSource.FromVisual( this );
    WpfMedia.Matrix transform = source.CompositionTarget.TransformToDevice;
    rect = new Wpf.Rect( rect.TopLeft, 
        new Wpf.Size( rect.Width * transform.M11, rect.Height * transform.M22 ) );
    base.OnWindowPositionChanged( rect );
}
</code></pre>
